{
  "rationale": "Evidence from iteration 1 (score 490.9, up from 107.4): the oval now finishes 20 of 20 runs with max_error 0.027, so the narrower sensor array and lower kp were right for gentle curves. The s-bend finishes 5 of 20 and the slalom finishes 0 of 20. In both failing tracks, 15 to 20 runs end off track at max_error about 0.057 to 0.062, which is the 0.06 m limit (max(0.06, 0.0125 + body_width/4) = 0.06 for any body_width up to 0.18, so the limit cannot be widened). Slalom runs show about one line-loss event each and steering_oscillation of 0.16. That pattern points to the robot overshooting sharp alternating bends and then losing the tape entirely, rather than to jitter on straights. Completion is worth 500 points and the time term is worth at most about 60, so staying on the line matters far more than speed.\n\nChanges: (1) base_speed 1.1 to 1.0. Less speed means less lateral momentum entering each bend. The oval should still finish, since its 1.9 s time at 1.1 leaves slack under the 3 s limit. (2) kd 0.5 to 0.8. Derivative action damps the fast signal swings in slalom alternations, which should reduce the overshoot that leads to line loss. (3) mass 0.8 to 0.6 kg. Lower yaw inertia (mass times body dimensions) lets the wheel differential change heading faster, so the same kp corrects bends with less lag. kp stays at 2.0 and ki at 0 to avoid confounding the changes. Geometry otherwise stays as in iteration 1, so the sensor array and track width are unchanged.\n\nExpected result: the oval still completes, with the s-bend completion rate rising above 25% and some slalom runs completing. The main risk is that lower speed pushes the oval time toward 2.3 s, which is still under 3 s.\n\nHypotheses: H1, the slalom failures come from overshoot at sharp alternating bends, so higher kd and lower inertia should cut max_error there. H2, speed is no longer the binding constraint on the oval, so a 1.0 base speed should still finish. Next measurements: per-track max_error and completion for s-bend and slalom, plus steering_oscillation, to tell whether kd or kp needs the next change. If slalom still leaves the line at one event, the next step is to test a wider sensor spread to catch the line sooner.",
